;; talk/kernel--structure.scm — structure lens on kernel.scm and apps.scm.

(objection 'o-kernel-structure-1 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'app-borrowing :lens "structure"
  :claim (canon 'launch-flows "The private and trader surfaces declared borrowing a gap and gave reasons: a loan makes the person owe the hub, and borrowed money mixed into a trader's own is the blended balance the trader refuses. The leaning app-borrowing then shows the debt in that same face's holdings and is opened from that face, so the reason for the gap is undone and only the build is moved. The decision d-apps-borrowing lists trader-surface and private-surface as the other options, but the chosen one is those two with an extra program to build in both wallets. The cost to the person is the refused risk arriving in the face they chose not to carry it, now with a second door to open it. What the rule asks for is that borrowing not be left out silently; it does not say where the debt shows, and that is the choice the decision never states."))

(objection 'o-kernel-structure-2 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-kernel-how-many :lens "structure"
  :claim (as-said 'several-surfaces "The decision admits that only two differences reach the kernel, one signer against a board and a runtime on the device against one on a server, and still leans to four. Its stated reason is that under d-kernel-programs a face costs little, but d-kernel-programs depends on d-kernel-how-many and its leaning is written as four faces. Each decision is the evidence for the other, and neither is argued alone. The options below four are also thin: one-with-pro-switch is a rejected release-queue item, and two-by-authority is dismissed in a clause. The cost is that the number every later file builds on rests on a loop, and the changes-if can only be tested after the faces exist."))

(objection 'o-kernel-structure-3 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-kernel-app-door :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "The floor says letting a program act needs a fresh yes each time, and floor-rules says it is never covered by a standing rule. app-trading-program signs orders at once inside the trader's limits, so the program acts without a fresh yes each time. Either 'letting a program act' means the one-time grant of access, which the text does not say, or a program is asked about every order and the trading program cannot exist. The door also gives a program the whole of the face's limits, so a program the trader tried for one afternoon can spend what the trader may spend by hand; a separate limit for programs is the middle choice, and it is neither an option nor a default. The person cannot tell what they are agreeing to when they let a program in."))

(objection 'o-kernel-structure-4 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'k-default-floor :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "This default writes the floor-rules leaning into the kernel before that decision is made: it says a credit line or a borrower approval needs a yes unless a capped standing rule covers it, which is the option some-kinds-by-rule. Anyone who picks no-exception in d-kernel-floor-rules finds the default already says otherwise. The default also names approving a borrower, which the floor in d-kernel-consent leaves out, so the two lists of what always asks differ. The cost is a hub operator who reads the default as settled when the argument over it, including the user's own decider status, is still open."))

(objection 'o-kernel-structure-5 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'k-question-screen :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "d-kernel-consent leans to asking by what a signature changes for the signer: what I hold, owe, may lose. This screen shows a list of per-transaction figures instead: most debited on a payment, fee on a collateral request, credit granted, fee and minimum on an order, can send. It is built around the kinds of signature, which is the each-face-alone option the decision refused, and it has no figure for the effects the decision names: ends a relationship, reaches many accounts, publishes something about me. The question a person meets before signing is therefore the one place where the by-effect choice is not carried through, and the three effects that reach most accounts or make something public are not shown."))

(objection 'o-kernel-structure-6 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'k-coverage :lens "structure"
  :claim (canon 'recovery-coverage-visible "The release queue wants an account-level view of whether recovery is covered and whether the watching service accepted or failed. d-kernel-evidence decides that the kernel says 'not covered' on native TRON and for boards, and that decision is argued carefully, but the screen that carries it shows nothing and offers nothing, as does k-programs for d-kernel-app-door. The two decisions whose whole point is to tell the person something they cannot see are given no figure to show it. The person who lost a device learns it is not covered when they try to leave, in the flow that cannot continue, which is the outcome the decision exists to prevent."))
